<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1976">1976</a>–<a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as LTTE, Tamil Tigers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam were a Tamil separatist militant organisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, founded in 1976 by Velupillai Prabhakaran to create an independent Tamil state, Tamil Eelam, in the north and east. During the civil war from 1983 they controlled large areas, ran a de facto administration and used suicide bombings and child soldiers. The Sri Lankan army defeated them in May 2009, when Prabhakaran was killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liberation-tigers-of-tamil-eelam.jpg" alt="Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tyiqnqnenq · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tamil_Eelam_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Measures such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinhala-only-act">Sinhala Only Act</a> and anti-Tamil riots gave rise to separatist ideas among many Tamil leaders. Velupillai Prabhakaran cited the anti-Tamil pogrom of 1958 as one of the factors that led him to militancy. In 1975 he assassinated the Mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a>, Alfred Duraiappah, in revenge for the incident at the Tamil conference of 1974. The LTTE was founded on 5 May 1976 as the successor to the Tamil New Tigers, as a reaction to the Constitution of 1972, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> the primary religion and Sinhala the national language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Black July anti-Tamil pogrom of 1983, Tamil militant groups grew dramatically and a full-scale insurgency began, the start of the Sri Lankan civil war. By 1986 the LTTE was the dominant Tamil militant group. An Indian Peace Keeping Force sent under the Indo-Sri Lanka Accord of 1987 left in March 1990. A ceasefire that held from June 1989 to June 1990 broke down when the LTTE massacred 600 police officers in the Eastern Province. A Ceasefire Agreement was signed in March 2002, monitored by Nordic countries.</p><p>At its peak in 2000 the LTTE held 76 per cent of the landmass of the Northern and Eastern provinces. After the election of Mahinda Rajapaksa as president in 2005 the LTTE pulled out of peace talks, and by August 2006 full-scale war had resumed. Prabhakaran led the organisation from its start until his death in 2009. At least 100,000 people were killed in the civil war between 1983 and 2009, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils">Sri Lankan Tamils</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>From a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> force the LTTE grew to resemble a conventional army, with a navy, an airborne unit, an intelligence wing and a suicide attack unit, the Black Tigers. It assassinated political opponents, and a suicide bomber killed the Indian prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajiv-gandhi">Rajiv Gandhi</a> on 21 May 1991. It was accused of attacks on civilians, of recruiting children, and of expelling Sinhalese and Muslim inhabitants from areas under its control, which the Sri Lankan Defence Ministry and Human Rights Watch have described as ethnic cleansing.</p><p>In its territory the LTTE ran a de facto state with Kilinochchi as its administrative capital, providing courts, police, a bank and radio and television stations. It presented itself as a secular national liberation movement. Countries including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, designated it a terrorist organisation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A suicide bomber from the <b>Tamil Tigers</b> killed the former Indian prime minister at an election rally in Sriperumbudur.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-21">21 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Jayewardene's government opened the economy, but anti-Tamil riots in 1983 began a civil war with the <b>Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam</b> seeking a Tamil state in the north and east.</blockquote><a href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></li></ul></section>
